`record -o` and `snapshot -o` keep what they wrote (#514). They used to open
their output with a truncating create, so re-running yesterday's command line
during an incident destroyed yesterday's capture — the one artifact either
verb exists to keep. An existing file is now refused, by name, **before any
session opens**: exit 2, this tool refusing what was typed (`exit.rs`). The
bus here is the dead one, so a refusal that came after the session would show
as a session error instead.

```console
$ zenctl record 'v1/**' -o yesterday.zrec --zenoh-config dead-bus.json5
? 2
Error: --out yesterday.zrec: the file already exists — pass --overwrite to replace it

$ zenctl record 'v1/**' -o yesterday.zrec --on dropped --pre 5 --zenoh-config dead-bus.json5
? 2
Error: --out yesterday.zrec: the file already exists — pass --overwrite to replace it

$ zenctl snapshot 'zk2/**' -o yesterday.zsnap --zenoh-config dead-bus.json5
? 2
Error: --out yesterday.zsnap: the file already exists — pass --overwrite to replace it

```

`--overwrite` gets past the refusal, and the run goes on to the bus — which
here is dead, so it stops at the session (exit 2, nothing asked). The file is
created only once a session is open, so a dead bus leaves both captures as
they were: `output-overwrite.out` pins them byte for byte.

```console
$ zenctl record 'v1/**' -o yesterday.zrec --overwrite --zenoh-config dead-bus.json5
? 2
Error: failed to open the Zenoh session
...

$ zenctl snapshot 'zk2/**' -o yesterday.zsnap --overwrite --zenoh-config dead-bus.json5
? 2
Error: failed to open the Zenoh session
...

```

Something that is not a regular file has no capture to destroy: `/dev/null`
is written to as it stands, without `--overwrite`.

```console
$ zenctl record 'v1/**' -o /dev/null --zenoh-config dead-bus.json5
? 2
Error: failed to open the Zenoh session
...

```
